WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.197 --> 00:00:03.090
The fourth case is zero times infinity.

2
00:00:03.183 --> 00:00:05.317
Take x multiplied by ln of one over

3
00:00:05.317 --> 00:00:08.333
x, as x approaches zero from the right.

4
00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.390
Here ln means the natural logarithm.

5
00:00:10.555 --> 00:00:13.243
x tends to zero, but ln of one

6
00:00:13.243 --> 00:00:15.640
over x tends to positive infinity.

7
00:00:15.791 --> 00:00:18.095
We must rewrite the expression,

8
00:00:18.095 --> 00:00:20.064
rather than immediately conclude zero.

9
00:00:20.314 --> 00:00:22.692
Set t equal to one divided by x.

10
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:24.616
Then t tends to positive infinity,

11
00:00:24.616 --> 00:00:27.268
and the expression becomes ln t divided by t.

12
00:00:27.603 --> 00:00:29.707
We now use a standard limit.

13
00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:32.045
ln t divided by t tends to zero.

14
00:00:32.195 --> 00:00:34.328
This result comes from the lesson

15
00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:37.520
on logarithms. It is not a rule that every

16
00:00:37.528 --> 00:00:39.099
zero-times-infinity form equals zero.

17
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.863
The limit in this example is therefore zero.

18
00:00:41.991 --> 00:00:44.807
The same calculation shows that x times ln x

19
00:00:44.807 --> 00:00:46.292
also tends to zero,

20
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.756
but through negative values. We will need this result

21
00:00:49.756 --> 00:00:50.966
in the sixth case.
